# Running Local Agentic Model with Ollama
::

<br/>

Lately I was into running local model to get help with coding and Ollama made it very easy in 3 steps:

1. Install Ollama
2. Download and run model
3. Install VS code extension

<br/>

Few things I learned, for agent mode, some VS code extensions require the model to be tagged with `tools` capability. For example, `deepseek-coder` is not tagged with `tools` even if it is meant for coding and thus it doesn't work with some VS code extensions, while `qwen-coder` models are tagged. 

<br/>

Another thing to watch out for is the context window. Each model has a suggested size, but 32k tends to be a good start.

<br/>

Most VS code extensions that I tried are not responsive enough. Some probably due to **System Prompt** which some extensions allow you to change. I can't say I have a favorite one yet due to poor performance. VS code extensions that I tried:

- continue.dev
- ROO code
- Cline

<br/>

I do most coding in Windows machine, however, my machine can't handle local model very well. So, I tried running Ollama in my Macbook and tried to connect to it from my window machine. A quick test can be done by setting **OLLAMA_HOST** environment variable.

<br/>

On my Macbook, I set OLLAMA_HOST to `0.0.0.0:11434` and then run `ollama serve`. Ensure firewall does not interfere. Then get the ip address to the machine by running `ipconfig getifaddr en0` on the terminal.

<br/>

On my Window machine, I set the OLLAMA_HOST environment variable to the ip address of the Macbook, so it will be `<ip-address>:11434`. Then launch Ollama for a quick test.

<br/>

To get VS code to use remote Ollama, I tried with Cline and set the Ollama Url to the ip address of the Macbook along with the port that Ollama use. It works, but sadly not very performant yet.

<br/>

Another thing to warn is machine can be very hot when running Ollama, so I make sure it doesn't automatically start (especially on startup) in any of my machines.
